AB  - Medical RAG often underperforms because retrievers optimize semantic similarity instead of whether evidence can actually answer a clinical question. We propose an answerability-oriented weak-supervision framework in which an LLM teacher scores BM25-retrieved PubMed passages by their utility for producing the correct answer, and these signals are distilled into dense retrievers. Using 11,450 MedQA questions and 23.9M PubMed abstracts, the resulting retrievers improve RAG accuracy across Qwen2.5-72B, DeepSeek-R1, and GPT-5. The best MedQA result reaches 94.45%, and gains persist on a temporally isolated MedXpert benchmark.
