AB  - Large language models (LLMs) have advanced in medical question answering, yet ensuring reliability remains challenging. We study multi-agent debate (MAD), where multiple LLM agents critique and aggregate each other’s answers. Our game-theoretic analysis shows that majority voting improves accuracy, confidence-weighted aggregation (e.g., with an LLM judge) is optimal under calibration, and early stopping yields efficiency with little loss. We implement MAD for multiple-choice medical questions (MedQA) and evaluate it across five frontier language models, comparing single-shot prompting, chain-of-thought (CoT), and MAD with 2 or 3 agents. MAD with 3 agents consistently outperforms single-shot and CoT, reaching 90% accuracy on Claude Sonnet 3.7 (vs. 88% with CoT). Moreover, early consensus allows most debates to end in early rounds, reducing latency. These results align with theory and suggest MAD as a promising path toward more accurate and trustworthy medical QA systems.
ER  - 

