AB  - Standardizing medical examination reports is essential for large-scale and AI-driven health analysis, yet multi-center settings face challenges such as terminology variation and strict data privacy. Although large language models (LLMs) generalize well across heterogeneous data, their use under privacy constraints remains limited. This study introduces and evaluates two privacy-preserving collaboration paradigms (model fusion and result fusion), to enable multi-center predictions without data sharing. Our distribution-aware framework integrates knowledge from decentralized models or their predictions. On a dataset of 900,000 reports from five training and six test hospitals across China, the proposed KL-Weighted Confidence Fusion achieves accuracies of 91.7% (±3.1%) for department-level and 91.0% (±5.2%) for item-level standardization, outperforming single-center models by up to 14.9% and matching the performance of a data-shared global model (92.1%). By using KL divergence to adaptively weight contributions from each center, our approach enables robust and privacy-protected integration of distributed medical data. This work enhances the scalability and practicality of multi-center medical AI while fully preserving data privacy.
ER  - 

