AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) perform strongly on straightforward medical questions. However, they often struggle with queries that are either highly specialty-specific or span multiple clinical disciplines. We developed a multi-specialty mixture-of-experts framework inspired by real clinical workflows. First, a dispatcher determines which one or more specialties a question belongs to. Second, based on that routing decision, specialty-specific agents answer the question and provide a confidence score. Third, the system aggregates the results by voting, with confidence-based tie-breaking. The dispatcher is optimized with DSPy using few-shot prompt optimization to improve specialty assignment. All components use the open-weight Meta Llama 4 Maverick model. Our system achieved 87.51% accuracy on the MedQA dataset (USMLE-style questions). Beyond overall accuracy, we use the framework to examine routing behavior, specialty-level performance, and disagreement patterns across agents. We emphasize interpretability and transparency by exposing per-agent votes, confidence scores, and reasoning in the output.
