AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used for medical question answering, where understanding when to trust their outputs is critical. Recent work has shown that increasing the sampling temperature substantially raises textual diversity while having little effect on accuracy, suggesting that surface-level variability is a weak proxy for reliability. In this paper, we investigate whether this independence between temperature and correctness also holds when variability is measured in the semantic embedding space. We evaluate three LLMs, including general-purpose and medically specialized models, on MedQA and the medical subset of MMLU. For each question and temperature, we generate multiple stochastic responses, embed them with a sentence transformer, and compute the total convex hull area of clustered embeddings as a geometric measure of semantic dispersion. Across models and datasets, higher temperatures consistently increase semantic spread, yet accuracy remains largely unchanged. This extends prior findings from textual to semantic-manifold variability and reveals a form of semantic stability: LLMs tend to preserve their answer choice even as their expressed reasoning becomes more diverse. These findings support human-centered AI by linking uncertainty to human oversight in medical decisions. They also highlight limitations of diversity-based uncertainty measures and motivate future work on cluster-based analyses of LLM reasoning.
