AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown strong potential in medical question answering, but their challenge to generate hallucinated or outdated information poses serious risks in clinical settings. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) mitigates this issue by grounding responses in external knowledge; however, indiscriminate retrieval can introduce noise and degrade performance when internal knowledge is sufficient. This paper presents a selective retrieval strategy that dynamically determines whether external retrieval is required. A fine-tuned LLM is used as a query routing mechanism to choose between direct generation and retrievalaugmented generation, combined with a stratified retrieval pipeline over trusted medical sources. Experiments on MedQA-USMLE and MMLU-Medical show accuracies of 70.46% and 77.59% using LLaMA-3 8B, improving over direct generation by 8.72 and 5.60 percentage points, respectively.
