AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly being used in clinical decision support systems to improve healthcare services. In this paper, we carefully examine the performance of these models and analyze their capabilities in medical applications. We present comparative results and investigate the effectiveness of LLMs as reported and presented in recent benchmarking studies. Our reviews examine the performance of both open source LLM models such as DeepSeek, Qwen, Llama, MedGemma, and Baichuan-M2 and proprietary Large Language Models such as ChatGPT (including many different versions) and Anthropic as reported after being tested on standardized medical assessments such as the United States Medical Licensing Examination (USMLE) and other benchmarks. The reviews show that recent versions of LLMs are reported to have strong understanding of medical content achieving accuracies ranging from $84 \%$ to $95 \%$. Our study explores detailed comparative analysis and trade-offs between open and closed source models. Furthermore, we present careful analysis and evaluation of the specialized open-source models MedGemma and Baichuan-M2. We show that while closed-source models are still having better overall performance, open-source models are increasingly catching up and providing additional benefits in domains of privacy, customization, and local controlled deployments. Finally, we present the Clinical Readiness Assessment Framework (CRAF) which is an approach attempting to evaluate LLMs for clinical deployments.
