AB  - Fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) in federated settings presents a fundamental tension: the gradients and adapter parameters that encode task-specific knowledge are large enough to make communication the primary bottleneck, yet aggressive compression risks convergence degradation. This paper introduces FedLoRA-C, a communication-efficient framework for federated fine-tuning that combines Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) with a novel selective layer compression (SLC) strategy. SLC identifies and prioritizes the subset of transformer layers contributing disproportionately to task-specific adaptation using a gradient-based layer sensitivity scoring function, transmitting only the top-K% of layers at full precision while applying 4-bit quantization to the remainder. We evaluate FedLoRA-C on four NLP benchmarks (SST-2, MNLI, SQuAD 1.1, MedQA) across heterogeneous federated configurations simulating IID and non-IID data distributions with 10-100 clients. FedLoRA-C achieves within 1.4% of centralized fine-tuning accuracy while reducing per-round communication cost by 67.3% compared to standard FedAvg with LoRA. Ablation studies confirm that sensitivity-scored layer selection outperforms random selection by 2.2% at identical communication cost, with the advantage increasing under higher data heterogeneity. These results demonstrate that high-fidelity federated LLM fine-tuning is achievable under realistic bandwidth constraints.
ER  - 

