AB  - Despite large language models (LLMs) showing strong capabilities in general medical question answering, they still struggle with multi-domain knowledge coverage, consistent reasoning, and preventing hallucination in complex clinical conditions. This paper introduces a two-stage Classification—Debate Multi-Agent Framework that improves reasoning consistency, accuracy, and interpretability. In the first stage, the system performs MeSH-guided domain classification, routing medical multiple-choice questions into multiple LLM-based “medical specialist” agents, each of which has expertise in a specific medical subfield, such as anatomy, organism, diseases, etc. In the second stage, domain-specific agents independently generate answers and engage in structured debate rounds upon shared history, with the final decision determined by consensus or majority voting. Experiments on MedQA and MedMCQA demonstrate consistent accuracy gains across all model configurations. Results further show that fine-grained domain classification improves reasoning alignment, and that output format also significantly affects performance. Without requiring additional fine-tuning, retrieval augmentation, or complex pipelines, the proposed lightweight framework outperforms single-model baselines and several existing multi-agent systems, highlighting the effectiveness of integrating domain-aware classification with structured debate.
ER  - 

